    schemeCitation: "Marking points quoted from the SEC marking scheme, Chemistry 2020 Higher Level — © State Examinations Commission.",
    id: "chem-2020-hl-q5-d-ii", topicId: "chem-1-3", conceptId: "why-group-2-metals-are-reactive",
    section: "B", questionRef: "2020 HL Q5(d)(ii)",
    stem: "The reactivity of an element is determined by the number and arrangement of electrons around the nucleus in each of its atoms. Around 1913 Bohr proposed that electrons in an atom occupied energy levels. Later sub-levels and orbitals were introduced into atomic theory to account for certain experimental observations. The diagrams below are two different representations of the first six energy levels in the electron cloud of a hydrogen atom. Write s, p electron configurations for beryllium, neon, magnesium and krypton. Refer to these electron configurations to explain",
